      'Sixteen girls were cycling home from school in rural Bahraich when they heard a child calling out from inside a truck, and a man\u2019s voice working hard to quiet him. Any one of them, alone, might have kept pedalling. Fifteen years old is young to challenge a grown man on an empty road, and the cost of being wrong in public is a cost girls in that district learn early.',
      'But they had spent months in Swabhiman and in their children\u2019s rights forum talking about exactly this. What trafficking looks like when it is happening in front of you. What a child in the wrong vehicle sounds like. Who you call. They had rehearsed the shape of this moment without knowing they were rehearsing it.',
