      'The point of a bridge centre is that it is nearby and it is not a school. A child who left in Class 3 and is now eleven cannot be dropped back into Class 6 and told to keep up. The gap has to be closed somewhere that does not humiliate them while they close it.',
      'So the centres taught, but they also did the other work: teachers sat with parents about education, about child labour, about early marriage. Children sang, drew, acted, and took turns being the one who taught the class. None of that is decoration. A child returning after two years away needs to be treated as capable before they can act capable.',
      'The model ran a six-month bridge and then followed the child into the government school afterwards, which is the part most programmes skip. Enrolment without retention is a number, not a change.',
      'Notice who did what. The village provided the room. Families rearranged the household economics that had sent a child to work. Local educators, trained but from those same villages, did the teaching. DEHAT built the model, trained the teachers and held the link to the government schools.',
      'Sixty-seven rooms is not a system. But it is sixty-seven places where a child who had already been written off had somewhere to go, close enough to walk to, and someone expecting them.',
