    lead: 'A school with nothing in it still has a floor, four walls, a window and a corridor.',
    body: [
      'SPICE started from an idea that sounds slight until you watch it work: the building a child sits in can do some of the teaching. Grid the floor and it holds fractions. Mark the window frame and it holds letter formation. Measure out the corridor and it holds distance and estimation.',
      'This is not decoration and it is not a substitute for investment. Painting a number line on a wall changes nothing on its own. A teacher has to be confident enough to teach off it, the children have to be in the room often enough to use it, and the families have to have somewhere to raise it when they are not.',
      'So the approach came bundled: the changes to the space, plus teacher training, plus a library, plus enrolment drives, plus parent-teacher meetings, plus the community ownership the Right to Education framework assumes and rarely gets.',
      'Twelve teachers reported it became easier to demonstrate what they were teaching. 238 children were enrolled through the drives. Retention among participating learners held at 89 per cent, maintained by home visits and meetings with parents rather than by the paint.',
      'The reason to tell this story is the refusal to separate things that are usually separated. Infrastructure, teaching method and attendance are normally three different projects with three different budgets. Here they were treated as one problem, which is what they are.',
    ],
    pull: 'Painting a number line on a wall changes nothing on its own.',
    actTitle: 'The cheapest classroom upgrade there is',
    act: 'Paint, training and a library, in a school that already exists. It is the lowest-cost way to make a government classroom worth attending.',
