    dek: 'Families are usually made to navigate them separately. This couple planned for both, and one of the two did not arrive.',
    line: 'November 2011\u2013January 2012 \u00b7 Ghorawal block, Sonbhadra \u00b7 Human Protection',
    lead: 'A child exists to the health system the day she is born, and to the state only when someone files for it.',
    body: [
      'The mother was seven months pregnant when a DEHAT field animator first visited on 16 November 2011. The ASHA had shared that she was pregnant, and the conversation covered delivery at a health facility and immediate breastfeeding. She had received both tetanus-toxoid doses in use at the time.',
      'The couple already had two children. This was not a first encounter with parenthood but a decision to connect this pregnancy to formal services. On a follow-up visit on 17 December, both parents agreed the birth would take place at Sarwat sub-centre.',
      'On 23 January 2012 she gave birth to a girl at the sub-centre. Both were healthy, and she began breastfeeding within the hour. The father then went to the sub-centre and applied for the child\u2019s birth certificate.',
      'Those actions sit across two systems. The facility provided delivery care. Breastfeeding depended on the mother and the people around her. Civil registration required a separate application at a separate counter. The facilitator discussed the options and followed up; the couple made the plan and used the institutions. The ASHA and sub-centre staff were part of the pathway.',
      'One thing did not work. The payment due under Janani Suraksha Yojana had not reached her.',
      'That unresolved point stays in the story. Accessing one service does not mean every entitlement follows automatically, and a story that tidies this away teaches the wrong lesson.',
      'Birth registration is more than paperwork. It establishes legal identity and shapes later access to education, protection and public services. Putting it next to maternal and newborn care shows how the rights intersect: no family should have to choose between a safe care pathway and being recognised by the administrative system.',
    ],
    pull: 'A story that tidies this away teaches the wrong lesson.',
