      'Gangiya Devi farms about an acre in a forest-edge village where the choices are agriculture and wage work. Her household has nine members, and her husband\u2019s migration for work was for a long time how they met their needs. She was doing a farmer\u2019s work without access to information about public schemes, collective processes or biological methods \u2014 and without seeing herself as someone who could put a village concern in front of an institution.',
      'The formation of an Aajeevika Adhikar Sangathan changed the setting. Villagers began meeting monthly. They worked through the conditions shaping their livelihoods and the difference between a difficulty one household carries and an issue a group can pursue. DEHAT facilitated the group\u2019s formation, the recurring meetings and training for AAS leaders. The contribution was capacity, not substitution.',
      'Gangiya used the space actively, took on responsibility inside the organisation and was recognised as a leader. Training gave her practice at the specific skills that role needs: convening a meeting, isolating an issue, stating it clearly, and following it past the village boundary. She has represented community concerns at tehsil and district level on several occasions.',
      'Her learning also went into the field. She took training in biological agriculture and adopted the practices on her land, alongside better access to public schemes.',
      'What can be said most confidently is that her role expanded. She moved from attending meetings to enabling them, became someone neighbours bring questions to, and became willing to carry a collective issue into a public forum. Farming knowledge, entitlement awareness and leadership were not three separate achievements. Together they let a woman farmer take control of livelihood decisions and take part in how her village is governed.',
      'Her story also corrects a common misreading of capacity building. The training was not the change. The change was what she did with it \u2014 meeting other women regularly, applying learning on her own land, organising shared concerns and staying with an administrative process that moves slowly.',
