      'Rajkumari Devi\u2019s household depended heavily on daily wage labour. The family had agricultural land, but limited technical knowledge kept cultivation at subsistence. The uncertainty reached routine household needs and the children\u2019s schooling. She did not read that as proof that farming had no future. She started looking for ways to make better-informed decisions on the land she already had.',
      'Through farmer meetings and training she learned seed treatment, soil health, biological inputs, mulching, crop planning and diversification. These were options, not a prescription. She chose what to test and took responsibility for applying it: farmyard manure and jeevamrit, mulching, line-based cultivation, and crop selection weighed against season and market.',
      'Turmeric became the important trial \u2014 under one bigha, intercropped with colocasia, around \u20b960,000 net. A separate onion record from 2023\u201324 covers two biswa: two quintals, \u20b91,500 input cost, \u20b94,000 income, \u20b92,500 net. Different crops and different periods, so the figures do not add together. What they show is a pattern of deliberate small-plot experimentation.',
      'She went on to cultivate banana on two bighas and turmeric on one, and household income became more regular. The progression is the point: she observed results, adjusted the portfolio and expanded selectively rather than repeating a demonstration.',
      'Her public role grew alongside the farm. The meetings carried rights and entitlements content and created occasions to speak in village forums. She now encourages other women to learn and take up livelihood activities, and has been recognised locally for her farming.',
      'So this is not a yield story. She became a farmer who plans, records and can explain her decisions, which is what makes her experience usable by anyone else.',
