    color: '#6B2478', lite: '#CFA0DA',
    art: 'story-119',
    title: 'The same ten biswa, twice: onion below, bitter gourd above',
    dek: 'Owning land does not produce income if the land only works in one dimension for part of the year.',
    line: '2025 \u00b7 Hajaripurwa, Mihinpurwa \u00b7 Climate Justice',
    lead: 'Eighteen people were living off four bigha and whatever wage work could be found.',
    body: [
      'Ram Chandra\u2019s extended family is eighteen people on four bigha. For years wage labour was central to keeping the household going. That is the ordinary smallholder position: some land, but not enough return from it, because cultivation is concentrated in a few crops and the land sits underused for much of the season.',
      'He joined farmer-learning activities after a community facilitator introduced the programme in the village, and took several training sessions at Girijapuri on biological cultivation and mixed vegetable systems. One idea stayed with him: onion could occupy the ground while bitter gourd climbed a trellis above it. The plot would not have to choose.',
      'He tried it on ten biswa first. \u20b925,000 from the bitter gourd, \u20b910,000 from the onion \u2014 \u20b935,000 in reported gross receipts. That figure is receipts, not profit: the record carries no harvest quantities, prices, full input and labour costs or home consumption.',
      'What followed says more than the number. The family expanded the combination from ten biswa to one bigha. A household does not commit six times the land to a method it did not believe in.',
      'They also began cultivating more vegetables generally, and report a better price because buyers recognised the produce as biologically grown. He bought a motorcycle for \u20b916,000 and uses it to sell vegetables \u2014 an asset acquired as the enterprise grew, not a line item anyone can trace to a single crop.',
      'DEHAT did not cultivate or market this crop. It facilitated repeated learning and put one crop-design option on the table. The family allocated the land, built the trellis, managed two crops on different clocks, found the buyers and decided to scale.',
      'Expansion raises exposure too. If a crop fails or prices fall, more of the household is in it. Water demand, pest pressure and labour \u2014 particularly women\u2019s unpaid labour \u2014 all need watching across several seasons.',
    ],
