      'Household wastewater accumulating around homes and pathways stops being a household matter. It shapes movement, sanitation and the immediate environment, and the burden is not shared evenly \u2014 the people responsible for water, care and cleaning deal with the consequences repeatedly.',
      'In Narayan Tanda, community discussion identified wastewater management as a local priority. A drainage facility was considered, and there was no land available for the arrangement proposed.',
      'That detail is the story. The community was not handed a standard solution and asked to accept it. They had to work out what would actually fit the settlement\u2019s physical conditions.',
      'They proposed soak pits instead. A soak pit lets greywater infiltrate the ground at a designated point rather than sitting exposed beside a house or running through an open drain. Whether it works depends on soil, groundwater depth, maintenance and the kind of wastewater going into it.',
      'DEHAT\u2019s part was leadership development, village-level planning and the interfaces through which residents could put a priority in front of the relevant public system. The annual record places the soak-pit decision alongside women\u2019s increased participation in local governance, and attributes that to repeated leadership training.',
      'The community carried the issue from discussion to a locally adapted proposal, and the construction was recorded as completed.',
      'The valuable change is not that an organisation installed a structure. It is that residents identified a shared environmental problem, recognised why the first option would not work, argued for a feasible alternative and saw it built. That is climate-resilient planning at a modest scale: using knowledge of the place to shape the response.',
      'Completion is not the same as function. Soak pits need siting, maintenance and protection from solid waste. Whether waterlogging actually falls across seasons, and who maintains each pit, is the next thing worth knowing.',
