    art: 'story-074',
    title: 'Resilience is not a record yield. It is a wider range of choices',
    dek: 'Across the border villages, households are adding vegetables, pulses and spices to the staples. No single farmer is the story.',
    line: '2026 \u00b7 India\u2013Nepal border villages, Bahraich \u00b7 Climate Justice',
    lead: 'For a small farm, erratic weather is not an agricultural event. It is next season\u2019s cash, this year\u2019s debt and the question of whether someone leaves.',
    body: [
      'A poor harvest on a fragmented farm near forest and border land narrows the diet, raises the borrowing and makes migration feel like a decision that has already been made. Depending on a few crops concentrates that risk: one price fall, one pest episode, one failed rain.',
      'The pattern emerging across the programme villages is that households are increasingly cultivating vegetables, pulses and spices alongside the traditional crops. The individual stories in this collection \u2014 turmeric, lentil, tomato, okra, onion, cauliflower, trellis mixtures \u2014 are the human scale of it. None of them represents the whole change.',
      'Some households used a few biswa for vegetables. Others stacked crops vertically, put pulses between existing rows, or introduced spices that could be stored or processed rather than sold immediately. Kitchen plots produced food for the house and a modest surplus.',
      'Biological preparations and farmyard manure gave some farmers a way to cut purchased inputs. Local demonstrations let neighbours watch the method under conditions they recognised.',
      'These were farmer decisions throughout. Training, seed access, demonstration plots and exposure visits widened the menu. Families chose which risks to take and which practices to keep. Krishi Mitras and community resource persons moved knowledge sideways instead of down.',
      'Pulse and legume consumption improved while staple consumption stayed relatively stable, which suggests diversification is affecting what households eat and not only what they sell.',
      'A harvest can create cash, replace a purchase, supply next year\u2019s seed, feed the family or become material for the next crop. Several crops on several clocks spread the exposure. That is what resilience looks like in practice.',
      'It also adds labour, water demand and market risk. Women\u2019s unpaid work in this shift needs recording rather than assuming, and biological methods deserve field evidence rather than enthusiasm.',
    ],
