    art: 'story-075',
    title: 'The pests arrive on a Tuesday, and the trainer is not there',
    dek: 'Agricultural decisions happen when the rain is late or the nursery fails. The question is who in the village can answer at that moment.',
    line: 'Through 2026 \u00b7 Mihinpurwa and border villages, Bahraich \u00b7 Climate Justice',
    lead: 'Who holds the knowledge after the project activity, or the funding cycle, ends?',
    body: [
      'Demonstration plots on their own do not sustain anything. Decisions have to be made when pests appear, rain is delayed, a nursery fails or a buyer changes the price. An occasional session run from outside cannot be present for any of that.',
      'Communities answered it with local roles. Village members selected farmer-enablers \u2014 Kisan or Krishi Mitras. Women emerged as AAS leaders. Demonstration farmers hosted practical observation. Resource persons connected technical learning to scheme access and collective concerns.',
      'Training covered low-external-input agriculture, crop diversity, seed, water, biological inputs and pest management, and exposure visits let farmers see practices elsewhere. But it was the local enablers who made that knowledge discussable afterwards: in a monthly meeting, on a plot, during nursery preparation, when a family was weighing whether to risk a new crop.',
      'Peer learning changes the social structure of technical knowledge. A neighbouring farmer will show you the labour, the failure and the adjustment, not only the result. A woman leader can connect a crop question to a scheme or a Gram Sabha priority. A younger farmer can test whether an inherited practice and a new technique can sit together.',
      'The Aajeevika Adhikar Sangathans began as spaces to discuss livelihoods and rights. They came to carry local advocacy, governance participation, climate-adaptation learning and collective problem-solving \u2014 which matters because climate vulnerability is rarely solved inside a field boundary. Farmers need documentation, irrigation, social protection, market connections or a collective answer to an institutional problem.',
      'A local enabler is not a substitute extension worker or a spokesperson appointed from outside. The role works when it stays accountable to the collective and enables other members to act.',
      'None of this guarantees continuity. Local leaders carry unpaid workloads, gendered restrictions, political pressure and dependence on project inputs. Transparent selection, refresher learning, inclusion of the most marginal farmers and real links to public agriculture systems are what would make it durable.',
      'The change that can be claimed is that resilience knowledge is beginning to sit with people who will still be in the village next year. That is a foundation, not a finished job.',
    ],
