      'Across eight forest villages the education gap was structural. One government school, and that school had no building, against an assessed need for four schools for 1,178 school-age children. Nishad Nagar alone had 179 children who could not reach one; 78 more in Kailash Nagar; 278 across Bhawani Pur and Bichhia.',
      'The first response came from inside the settlements. Non-formal learning spaces, recorded as Child Right Centres, were set up closer to the children. One centre in Nai Basti ran on full community contribution.',
      'That matters because the families were not receiving an education activity. They were paying for a practical local arrangement while building the confidence to ask why the public system had not arrived.',
      'The centres were never treated as a permanent substitute for government responsibility. Education Right Committees were constituted in the villages. Through them, community members identified non-school-going and dropout children, discussed the absence of adequate schools, and submitted demands for schools or alternative centres to government officials.',
      'An Education Guarantee Scheme centre opened in Nishad Nagar. A locally sustained response had become a public provision.',
      'The method is worth naming: address the immediate gap without normalising it. Community action created continuity for children and generated a claim on the state at the same time.',
      'What lasts is the capability around the centre. Once parents could identify excluded children, sit on a committee and present a shared demand, education access stopped being a household problem and became a village question about public accountability.',
