      'The girls who joined were not treated only as students. A rotating Champs as a Teacher approach gave each of them responsibility for helping peers through a lesson. That requires speaking clearly, listening, noticing the point at which someone lost the thread, and finding another route to the same place. It is public confidence built by repetition rather than by a leadership event.',
      'Working women and supportive adults were invited in as mentor-mothers. Families and younger siblings watched girls travel, study, sit examinations and talk about streams and careers.',
      'Once that was visible, the case for a girl continuing did not rest on a facilitator’s assurance. The girls were the local evidence.',
